RISE ASCII SCENE 7
#
# proximity_mesh_contact -- the MESH-neighbour fixture for `proximity(r)`,
# the cross-object contact signal.  This is scene D of
# docs/CROSS_OBJECT_PROXIMITY_DESIGN.md 1, and the acceptance render for
# that document's Phase 2 (the closest-point query on an indexed mesh's own
# BVH).  It drives tests/MeshClosestPointTest section (g).
#
# WHY A SECOND FIXTURE SCENE AT ALL.  Scene C
# (proximity_closed_forms.RISEscene) covers every ANALYTIC family against
# algebra written by hand.  A mesh has no closed form, so what has to be
# proved here is different: that the signal reads CONTACT on real assets at
# real contact, and that the contact is real rather than asserted.  The
# showroom scene was the first choice for this and is REJECTED -- its torus
# knot floats 0.69 m above its platform, so no authorable radius reaches it
# and every check would have passed by reading the platform's own
# neighbours instead.
#
# ============================================================
# THE PLACEMENT NUMBERS BELOW ARE OWNED BY THE TEST, NOT BY ME
# ============================================================
#
# `MeshClosestPointTest` section (g) loads both meshes through the same
# deserialize this scene's `risemesh_geometry` chunks use, reads the vertex
# arrays and `GenerateBoundingBox()`, re-derives each offset below, and
# ASSERTS it against the number written here at 1e-6.  So if either asset is
# ever replaced or re-exported, the TEST FAILS -- rather than the bunny
# quietly floating a millimetre above the plane while every proximity check
# still passes for the wrong reason (it would then be reading the plane's
# other neighbours).  Do not hand-edit a position here without re-running
# that test and updating its constants in the same commit.
#
#   bunny    scale 1.  Its bounding box floor is y = +0.0329874, which is
#            also its lowest VERTEX (the box is computed in double from the
#            vertex array, so the two agree exactly -- the test checks that
#            too).  Offsetting by the negative of it puts that vertex ON the
#            plane y = 0:
#
#                position   0  -0.0329874  0
#
#            Its lowest vertex is at object (x, z) = (-0.053835, 0.0179508)
#            -- a FOOT, not the model centre, which is why the gate's probe
#            stations are computed from the vertex array and not from the
#            bounding box's midpoint.
#
#   dragon   scale 0.35, placed so ITS lowest vertex lands exactly on the
#            BUNNY's highest vertex in world space.  Deliberately NOT "the
#            dragon's bbox floor onto the bunny's bbox ceiling": that only
#            makes the two BOXES touch and leaves the two SURFACES an
#            unknown distance apart, so the mesh-on-mesh station would be
#            asserting an ordering instead of a number.  Vertex onto vertex
#            is one point of genuine contact, where the true distance is
#            exactly 0 and the signal must read exactly 1.
#
#                position   -0.0318315  0.135862595  -0.014760295
#
#            It balances on a single point.  That is geometrically honest
#            and physically silly, and it is the right trade for a fixture:
#            what is being pinned is a distance, not a pose.
#
# WHAT EACH OTHER OBJECT PROVES:
#
#   plane        InfinitePlane at y = 0.  THE RECEIVER, and a family that
#                publishes no signal provider of its own -- `proximity` is
#                the one signal that works on any receiver.  Its material is
#                keyed on `proximity(0.02)` (2 cm, a WORLD length) so the
#                render shows the seam directly.
#
#   nocast       A sphere of radius 0.05 centred at (-0.6, 0.053, 0) with
#                `casts_shadows FALSE`, so its underside is 3 mm above the
#                plane.  It COUNTS: `casts_shadows` is a light-visibility
#                flag and this signal is geometry presence.  Directly
#                beneath it the plane reads the closed form
#                1 - 0.003/0.02 = 0.85, which the test asserts at 1e-6.
#
#   key_panel    A `rect_light` 2 mm above the plane at (0.6, 0.002, 0),
#                facing down.  EMITTERS NEVER COUNT -- a light panel parked
#                millimetres off a wall must not paint grime on it.  The
#                number that makes this check mean something: if emitters
#                DID count, the plane under it would read
#                1 - 0.002/0.02 = 0.9.  The test asserts EXACTLY 0.
#
# NOT IN THIS SCENE, stated so nobody infers coverage: a displaced-geometry
# neighbour (Phase 2 ships that forward, and `MeshClosestPointTest` (d)
# pins it directly on the geometry rather than through a scene), a RAW mesh
# (scene C already carries the refusal), and per-sample motion blur.
#
# THE RENDER is the Phase-2 acceptance picture: grime gathers where the
# bunny's feet meet the plane, under the shadowless sphere, and NOT under
# the light panel.  A frame where the panel has a dark halo under it is the
# emitter rule broken; a frame with no darkening at the feet is the mesh
# query refusing.
#

##################################################
# Painters and materials
##################################################

#
# The receiver's grime: a clean pale floor lerped toward a warm brown as
# the signal rises -- the actual authoring idiom the signal exists for, not
# a false-colour ramp, so the acceptance render reads as a picture.
#
# IT IS TINTED, NOT MERELY DARKENED, and that is a legibility decision made
# after looking at the frame.  A grime term that only multiplies the albedo
# down produces a dark band under the bunny that is visually
# indistinguishable from the directional key's own contact SHADOW -- which
# is precisely the confusion this whole signal exists to resolve (design
# 2: an occlusion cannot draw this seam, and a picture where the two look
# alike proves nothing to a reader).  A hue shift separates them: the
# shadow stays neutral grey, the grime goes brown.
#
expression_painter
{
	name			p_floor
	def				grime	proximity(0.02)
	expr			vec3( 0.74, 0.72, 0.68 )*( 1.0 - grime ) + vec3( 0.20, 0.13, 0.08 )*grime
}

lambertian_material
{
	name			m_floor
	reflectance		p_floor
}

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			p_mesh
	color			0.62 0.58 0.52
}

lambertian_material
{
	name			m_mesh
	reflectance		p_mesh
}

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			p_sphere
	color			0.30 0.45 0.62
}

lambertian_material
{
	name			m_sphere
	reflectance		p_sphere
}

##################################################
# Geometries
##################################################

infiniteplane_geometry
{
	name			g_plane
	xtile			1.0
	ytile			1.0
}

risemesh_geometry
{
	name			g_bunny
	file			models/risemesh/bunny.risemesh
}

risemesh_geometry
{
	name			g_dragon
	file			models/risemesh/dragon_small.risemesh
}

sphere_geometry
{
	name			g_sphere
	radius			0.05
}

##################################################
# Objects
##################################################

standard_object
{
	name			plane
	geometry		g_plane
	material		m_floor
	orientation		-90 0 0
	position		0 0 0
}

#
# Lowest vertex ON the plane.  See the header: the offset is
# -(bounding box floor) and the test re-derives it from the asset.
#
standard_object
{
	name			bunny
	geometry		g_bunny
	material		m_mesh
	position		0 -0.0329874 0
}

#
# Lowest vertex ON the bunny's highest vertex.  See the header.
#
standard_object
{
	name			dragon
	geometry		g_dragon
	material		m_mesh
	position		-0.0318315 0.135862595 -0.014760295
	scale			0.35 0.35 0.35
}

#
# Underside 3 mm above the plane; counts despite casts_shadows FALSE.
#
standard_object
{
	name			nocast
	geometry		g_sphere
	material		m_sphere
	position		-0.6 0.053 0
	casts_shadows	FALSE
}

##################################################
# Lights
##################################################

#
# THE FIXTURE LIGHT, and the one whose PLACEMENT is the point: 2 mm above
# the plane.  Its exitance is low because at 2 mm it is effectively lying on
# the floor and would otherwise blow out the whole frame; it is here to be
# a NEIGHBOUR that must not count, and only incidentally to light anything.
#
rect_light
{
	name			key_panel
	center			0.6 0.002 0
	size			0.06 0.06
	facing			0 -1 0
	color			1.0 0.95 0.88
	exitance		4.0
}

#
# The actual key.  `direction` is the vector FROM the surface TO the light
# (docs/SCENE_CONVENTIONS.md 4), so this is a high key over the camera's
# shoulder.
#
directional_light
{
	name			key
	power			1.5
	color			1.0 0.98 0.94
	colorspace		Rec709RGB_Linear
	direction		0.35 0.85 0.40
}

ambient_light
{
	name			amb
	color			0.32 0.34 0.40
	colorspace		Rec709RGB_Linear
	power			0.9
}

##################################################
# Camera and output
##################################################

standard_shader
{
	name			global
	shaderop		DefaultPathTracing
}

pathtracing_pel_rasterizer
{
	samples			16
	pixel_filter	box
	oidn_denoise	FALSE
}

file_rasterizeroutput
{
	pattern			rendered/proximity_mesh_contact
	type			PNG
	bpp				8
	color_space		sRGB
}

film
{
	width			640
	height			360
}

pinhole_camera
{
	location		0.250 0.175 0.310
	lookat			-0.020 0.058 0.0
	up				0 1 0
	fov				46.0
}
